    pr_ref:  # 4a73
    description: "Relax the LOCAL coverage target from 100% to ~95%, and ROTATE which lines go\nuncovered using a logged random seed, so that over successive runs every line\nis still exercised while any single run is much cheaper.\n\nWAIT TO IMPLEMENT (owner, 2026-08-14). Filed to preserve the design.\n\nWHY IT IS WORTH REAL MONEY, on the corrected census. The last 1% of coverage\ncosts 8.8 minutes and 140 tests -- 3.8s per test against a suite mean of\n0.24s -- because the final statements are reachable only through the heavy\nfixture-bearing integration tests. Full trajectory to 100% is 21.7 min serial;\nto 95% it is 12.5 min. NOTE this SUPERSEDES the earlier \"95% and 100% are 11\nseconds apart\" reading, which came from the pre-fix index and was wrong.\n\nWHY IT IS DANGEROUS, and the reason it is not obviously a good trade. Those\n140 tests are precisely the ones you least want to skip: this repo's two\nnear-misses of the week both passed every unit test and were caught only\nend-to-end. And the index records EXECUTION, not ASSERTION -- a line run by\nten tests that assert nothing about it reads as covered ten times and\nprotected zero. A cost-ranked skip list preferentially drops the tests that\ncatch this project's characteristic bugs.\n\nWHAT MAKES IT TRACTABLE. greedy_trajectory already takes `epsilon` and a\n`seed`, sampling among near-ties where the ordering is arbitrary anyway, and\ndeterminism is already load-bearing and tested (Python hash randomisation\nwould otherwise make one seed produce different trajectories per process). So\nrotation is a logged seed plus a persisted record of which residual was\nskipped, not new search machinery.\n\nNON-NEGOTIABLE: CI's gate stays at 100%. This is a local-loop accelerator, and\nthe rotation is only sound because the full gate still runs somewhere."  # 4a73
    fields: {}  # 4a73
- op: discuss  # 8b49
